{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["The sense of <b>אַנְשֵׁי חַיִל</b> is: men fit to lead a great people, for every assembled body or multitude is called חַיִל, and it is not limited to those who go forth to army of war alone. Thus, 'חַיִל גָּדוֹל מְאֹד' (Ezekiel 33:6), and concerning locusts, 'חֵילִי הַגָּדוֹל' (Joel 2:25), and concerning money, 'כֹּחִי וְעֹצֶם יָדִי עָשָׂה לִי אֶת הַחַיִל הַזֶּה' (Deuteronomy 8:17), 'יִשְׂאוּ עַל כֶּתֶף עֲיָרִים חֵילֵיהֶם' (Isaiah 30:6), and concerning fruits, 'תְּאֵנָה וָגֶפֶן נָתְנוּ חֵילָם' (Joel 2:22). Now a man may be called אִישׁ חַיִל in matters of law — the wise, diligent, and upright one. In war — the mighty, diligent one who knows the formations of battle. And a woman too is called 'אֵשֶׁת חַיִל' (Proverbs 31:10) when she is diligent and knowledgeable in managing the household.\nNow Jethro spoke in general and in particular. He said that one should seek out men fit to lead the great people in matters of law — that is the general statement — and then he specified: they should be <b>יִרְאֵי אֱלֹהִים אַנְשֵׁי אֱמֶת וְשֹׂנְאֵי בָצַע</b>, for they would not be men of חַיִל in law without these qualities. There was no need to mention their wisdom and understanding, for it is self-evident that this is included in אַנְשֵׁי חַיִל. And when it says below, <b>וַיִּבְחַר מֹשֶׁה אַנְשֵׁי חַיִל</b>, everything is included in that general term: they were <b>יִרְאֵי אֱלֹהִים אַנְשֵׁי אֱמֶת שֹׂנְאֵי בָצַע וַחֲכָמִים וּנְבוֹנִים</b>. Furthermore, since it says <b>מִכָּל יִשְׂרָאֵל</b>, the meaning is: the choice ones from all Israel — and these are they in whom all these qualities are found. For once it says <b>מִכָּל יִשְׂרָאֵל</b>, it means that they were the select from among all of them; and it is well known that the best of Israel have all good qualities in them. But Jethro, since he was not familiar with the people, needed to specify.\nThere are interpreters who explain 'אַנְשֵׁי חַיִל' as men of strength and diligence, and likewise 'אֵשֶׁת חַיִל' — a woman of strength and diligence in the work of the household, as will be explained in its place (Proverbs 31:10), in the sense of 'וַאֲשֶׁר כֹּחַ בָּהֶם לַעֲמֹד בְּהֵיכַל הַמֶּלֶךְ' (Daniel 1:4), and similarly 'הֲנִיעֵמוֹ בְּחֵילְךָ' (Psalms 59:12) — in your strength, and 'וּבְרֹב חֵילוֹ לֹא יְמַלֵּט' (Psalms 33:17) — from the Aramaic expression, for in 'יֶשׁ לְאֵל יָדִי' (Genesis 31:29) Onkelos renders 'אִית חֵילָא בִּידִי'. And below (Exodus 18:25) it says <b>וַיִּבְחַר מֹשֶׁה אַנְשֵׁי חַיִל מִכָּל יִשְׂרָאֵל</b> — the chosen from all the people, and everything is included, as I have explained." "<b>שׂוֹנְאֵי בָצַע</b> — that they hate their own money in a lawsuit, as in that which we say (Bava Batra 58b): any judge who causes money to be taken from someone through judgment is no judge. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 18:21). He means to explain that any money which they know a person could take from them through legal proceedings, they would hate and return it of their own accord, even if it is truly theirs — for example, one who acquired a slave without witnesses, and similar cases. But the language in the Mekhilta is not so. Rather, it is stated there thus: 'שׂוֹנְאֵי בָצַע' — that they hate to receive money in judgment; these are the words of Rabbi Yehoshua. Rabbi Elazar ha-Moda'i says: 'שׂוֹנְאֵי בָצַע' — these are those who hate their own money; if they hate their own money, all the more so the money of others. Rabbi Yehoshua explained <b>שׂוֹנְאֵי בָצַע</b> as haters of bribery, as in 'כֻּלּוֹ בּוֹצֵעַ בָּצַע' (Jeremiah 6:13), 'אִישׁ לְבִצְעוֹ מִקָּצֵהוּ' (Isaiah 56:11). Rabbi Elazar ha-Moda'i interpreted it as: they are those who hate much money and have no desire for an abundance of silver and gold, in the sense of 'אִם אֶשְׂמַח כִּי רַב חֵילִי וְכִי כַבִּיר מָצְאָה יָדִי' (Job 31:25). For money is called בֶּצַע: 'מַה בֶּצַע כִּי נַהֲרֹג אֶת אָחִינוּ' (Genesis 37:26), 'וְאִם בֶּצַע כִּי תַתֵּם דְּרָכֶיךָ' (Job 22:3), 'וְהַחֲרַמְתִּי לַה׳ בִּצְעָם וְחֵילָם לַאֲדוֹן כָּל הָאָרֶץ' (Micah 4:13).\nFurthermore, I saw in the Yalkut Shimoni: <b>שׂוֹנְאֵי בָצַע</b> — that they hate their own money, and needless to say the money of others. They would say: even if he burns my grain stack, even if he cuts down my plantings, I judge him as fit. And this, the meaning of hating one's own money as stated by Rabbi Elazar ha-Moda'i, is that they would not spare their own money in the matter of judgment if loss should come to them from it. And Moses further admonished them on this: <b>לֹא תָגוּרוּ מִפְּנֵי אִישׁ</b> (Deuteronomy 1:17). And בֶּצַע is money, as I have explained.\nOnkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 18:21) renders: 'דְּסָנַן לְקַבָּלָא מָמוֹן' — and money in his rendering is not bribery, but rather that they should not accept money from people, whether as a gift or as a loan, ever, so that they not show favoritism at the time of judgment. As in that which they said (Ketubot 105b): a judge who accepts a question — it is forbidden to judge before him. And according to the plain sense, <b>אַנְשֵׁי אֱמֶת שֹׂנְאֵי בָצַע</b> means that they love truth and hate oppression, and when they see oppression and violence their spirit cannot bear it, but all their desire is to rescue the stolen goods from the hand of the oppressor."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {1 "The Aramaic phrase דְּסָנַן לְקַבָּלָא מָמוֹן is from Onkelos's translation of this verse; I have left it in Aramaic script as Ramban quotes it. The phrase 'a judge who accepts a question' (הַאי דַּיָּנָא דְּשָׁאֵל שְׁאֵלְתָא) renders a Talmudic passage that is deliberately hyper-literal; the reference is to a judge who receives any benefit before ruling."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:17:47.417751136Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}